A||||21||Fformula88||More hatred for the Pontiac Dealer||||8||
Z||000000||Fformula88||10-17-2001||12:54 PM|||| <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/mad.gif">  Maybe GM's biggest problem selling cars are their dealer network!  Every time I have to deal with them (and I make that as little as conceivably possible) they give me more reasons to despise them!<P>On Sept 22, I ordered a couple parts for my Fiero.  I had gotten the part numbers for the floor pan plugs... the metal ones... that go under the passenger compartment carpeting.  One of the four on my car has mysteriously rusted away, while the others are rust free  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smiley_wtf.gif">  I decided to order a complete set.  Well, its now Oct 17 and they are still waiting for the smaller plugs!!  Last Friday they said they were being shipped and would be here "any day".  I guess any day could mean next year for Pontiac!!  I am just glad I am not waiting for something needed for the car to operate since its almost a month already!!<P>I had to rant a bit!!I just cannot stand those dealers!!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/mad.gif"><P>------------------<BR>John  <BR>AOLIM: Fformula88<BR>      <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/formulasig.JPG">         <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/fullstaff.gif">   <P>1988 Fiero Formula<BR>1985 Fiero SE 2M6||||reg||8
Z||000001||Monkeyman||10-17-2001||02:14 PM||||Sorry, but I have to take issue with this.  It's not the dealer, it's the warehouse.  I can't speak for any dealers other than the local one here in Wabash, but the one here (Dorias Chevrolet) is outstanding.  When they order me a part, it's usually here right away.  If not, they let me know exactly what's going on including showing me any emails or letters they've received from GM Central (the warehouse or whatever it is).  And their service department is outstanding.  They always fit me in as soon as they can and try to cut me whatever kind of deal they can, too.  Of course, I'm there about twice a month on average.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">  (They know my voice on the phone.)<P>I'm not saying that your local dealership isn't screwing you around.  Just saying that not all are that way.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Fformula88||10-17-2001||04:20 PM||||Thats a good point... both are to blame here.  It took me 3 weeks to get the dealer to actually do more than pull my paperwork out of the drawer and actually find out the parts status.||||reg||1
Z||000003||NEPTUNE||10-17-2001||04:59 PM||||We get treated very well at Mcnamara Pontiac (Orlando). You just have to realize that Fieros are such a small part of their business you really can't expect them to fall all over themselves when you ask for an obscure part for a 15 year old out of production car. Try bringing donuts next time- or beer.  LOL.||||reg||7
Z||000004||Banzai||10-17-2001||05:09 PM||||What are the part #s for the body plugs?? Their Cost?? The one under my drivers seat is on it's way out. I will replace them when I get new carpet(wife said she'd get it for me for xmas). Thanks<P>Banzai !!!!!!!!!||||reg||1
Z||000005||Master_Sushi||10-17-2001||05:25 PM||||The plugs in my 84 were rubber. Were some of them rubber/metal or what? Im kind of confused now.||||reg||1
Z||000006||Fformula88||10-17-2001||05:51 PM||||I don't expect them to fall all over themselves, but do you really think a business should give bad service to people becuase they aren't part their biggest area of business?<P>People remember this type of service for a long time.  I would never buy a new from this dealer with the lack of interest they are showing me.  They may see me with someone with a 14 year old car buying an obscure part, and maybe they figure someone driving a 14 year old car probably won't give them much business.<P>Since I do not own or drive Fiero's exclusively, and have been shopping new cars to replace my daily driver (probably not until the spring... why drive a new one thru the salt?) they just became the last dealer on earth I'd by a car from.<P>Thats a lot of business to risk over $25 worth of parts.<P>I don't have the part numbers here... I will post them later.  6 plugs total ran about $25 (if they ever arrive!)||||reg||1
Z||000007||JohnF||10-17-2001||06:31 PM||||I agree with the fact that it is PEOPLE and the business that makes a difference.  Last Wed. a.m. I went to my Pontiac dealer and had to order $5.12 in parts.  I was told it would probably be the following Monday when they would arrive from the warehouse - I got them the very next day!<P>I'm still trying to figure out how to get them installed, Hehehehehe!||||reg||12
Z||000008||Formula||10-17-2001||06:47 PM||||Someone from a pontiac dealership actually drove an hour from carnegie PA to Waynesburg PA for my tie rod end.  They are going to get my service again!<P>Thats right, fastest shipping part I ever got, it ended up taking about an hour and a half.||||reg||1
Z||000009||Raydar||10-17-2001||10:03 PM||||I bought a set of dew wipes and a tensioner for my 88 Duke, from my local Pontiac dealer (Lou Sobh, in West Atlanta.) <BR>Dew wipes cost me $24 each, and the tensioner was $48. (List for the tensioner is around $64.)<BR>The parts guy actually invoiced them on <I>his</I> account. It appears that I paid <I>his</I> cost. Dew wipes were an unclaimed order. Were already there. The tensioner only took a day or two.<BR>Think I'll go back? You bet I will!<P>------------------<BR>Raydar - aka Steve<BR><FONT SIZE="1"><BR>Black 88 Formula.<BR>Red 88 Duke coupe. "The Project" <BR>88 Formula parts car. "The Donor"<BR></FONT>||||reg||14
Z||000010||Nashco||10-17-2001||10:55 PM||||I had a HORRIBLE experience at the dealership in town here trying to get my recall work done.  The first dealings I ever had with them were completely courteous, trying to get my car inspected.  After leaving the car outside for a night and day, they told me they looked at it and the recall had been done...but it had CLEARLY never been done.  So the guy had the guts to say, "Well, we've done a couple hundred of these, so we know what we're talking about."  I then asked him to look at the recall with me, then look at the car with me; after doing that, I let him take his foot out of his mouth; it didn't come out nearly as easy as it went in though! <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">  Long story short, after I finally got the work done eight months later, I will NEVER EVER go to that dealership, no matter the cost.  I drove to another town just to get transmission fluid!  I would happily drive a hundred miles each way to another dealership before going there ever again.<P>I know this isn't just this dealership, but there are good ones.  My dad worked at a Pontiac dealership near Seattle for a couple years doing recalls on Fieros, and said they gave away as much as was possible (where as I had to fight tooth and nail to get a thing); he kept telling me to report them to the BBB.  Ask around, and go to a dealership that will help you out, as others recommend.  Trust me, service is worth an extra drive!<P>Bryce<BR>88 GT||||reg||1
Z||000011||ChopTop||10-18-2001||09:55 AM||||Maybe GM's biggest problem selling cars are their dealer network! <P>Actually I think GM would agree with that assessment, and Ford, and everyone else too.  Since car manufacturers don't own the dealerships they can't tell them how to run their business, only influence them through financial rewards or the withholding of them.  <BR>I had a recent experience with my Jeep Wrangler, it wouldn't go into gear.  I hated the thought of taking it in to the dealership, but it was under warranty and a "5 Star" program dealership.  The short story, they were going to  charge me 400 bucks (warranty doesn't cover wear items), and charge Chrysler Corporation 800 bucks, what a deal!  Told em forget it, had it flatbeded to my mechanic, hour later, he added transmission fluid, 5,000 miles later and it's still running fine.  <BR>The dealer network is very strong, plus there's some law that prohibits car companies from selling cars directly to the public.  As long car companies can't control their dealers we will continue to be at the mercy of poor Stealerships.<BR>That's one reason I like this forum so much, we really do save each other time and money.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000012||btoth||10-18-2001||12:20 PM||||I haven't had any problems ordering parts from the local Chevy dealer... but the Pontiac dealership sucks so bad.  They hate any old cars, especially Fieros. (It's not my fault it was built by Pontiac!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/rolleyes.gif"> )  <P>I stopped going to them for anything after they "ran diagnostics" on my car ( = checking for codes with a computer) and came up with a million different codes.  $400 for this little service... which I was quite capable of doing myself!!  I said "screw you, I can check my own codes, I was looking for a 'professional' opinion on my stalling problems."  Morons.  A week later <B>I</B> figured out it was just a plugged catalytic converter. Haha.  That's when I learned to first investigate all the possibilities of a problem before I take it anywhere, and now I have a regular mechanic that actually thinks and lets me supply him with the parts.||||reg||1
Z||000013||Fformula88||10-18-2001||03:32 PM||||Those laws must be on a state basis since there are some company owned dealer outlets.  Didn't Ford buy out their Indianapolis dealers and start selling cars themselves as an "experiment'?  If I am remembering correctly, they were going to have just a few retail outlets and sprinkle the city with lots of factory repair shops so you had to go to the central location to buy the car but could get it serviced close by your home/work or whatever was convenient.  Any Indy Fiero owners can confirm or correct (no, not pace car people unless you live in Indy  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">)||||reg||1
Z||000014||Gerald||10-18-2001||06:17 PM||||I used to work at a GM service department. When I had to do my timing gears I got them to order all the parts and gaskets that i would need. I couldnt use a single gasket that the sent for. They were all wrong. 3 total. I had to cut one out of a sheet of gasket and silicone for the rest. I worked there and still got bad service. And it was a small town so all the inbreds at the dealership made fun of the car. Stupid hicks<P>------------------<BR>87 coupe 5 spd||||reg||1
Z||000015||Gerald||10-18-2001||06:18 PM||||I used to work at a GM service department. When I had to do my timing gears I got them to order all the parts and gaskets that i would need. I couldnt use a single gasket that the sent for. They were all wrong. 3 total. I had to cut one out of a sheet of gasket and silicone for the rest. I worked there and still got bad service. And it was a small town so all the inbreds at the dealership made fun of the car. Stupid hicks<P>------------------<BR>87 coupe 5 spd||||reg||1
Z||000016||Black86SE||10-18-2001||06:34 PM||||Well I have had good luck and bad with my "local" pontiac dealership. But I have had great luck with my Chevy dealership (for my truck). When I took my car into the pontiac dealer for the recall last month, they gave me a hard time and kinda really made me mad. When I picked my car up (the same time my dad bought a new bonneville ssei, and had really good sales service) I went to the parts dept. to get a battery hold down block. The guy in there was really nice and I wouldn't mind going back there to get parts (if they can get what I need). Sometimes it isn't the whole dealer as the individual people who you deal with.<P>------------------<BR>1986 V6 SE<BR>Black<BR>17" Wheels<BR>P235/45/ZR17 <BR>BF Goodrich g-Force KDWS<p>[This message has been edited by Black86SE (edited 10-18-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000017||Fformula88||10-18-2001||08:14 PM||||Just an update...<P>Only 5 days shy of one month the Pontiac dealer FINALLY got these things in.  To their defense, I am not sure its totally their fault now that I see them.  The larger plugs look an identical metal, but the smaller ones are definately newer, and maybe better.  They are galvanized a different way that I can tell from looking at the.  They also have a more modern GM part number sticker on them vs the old ones.  If they are still producing these things, they must have used them on a car since the Fiero.  Maybe they were out of stock.<P>Now I just have to figure out when I am going to tear the car apart to put them in.  I only really need one replaced, but figured if I am going to tear the car apart I might as well replace them all with new ones.<P>Part numbers for those who are interested:<BR>Small - 2057-9995<BR>Large - 1021-5806||||reg||1
Z||000018||Fformula88||10-18-2001||08:34 PM||||I posted some pictures of the new caps in the tech section in response to an older thread there where we were wondering about the availability of the plugs.  If your unsure what they are, take a look!!<P> <A HREF="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum2/HTML/014555.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum2/HTML/014555.html</A> <BR>||||reg||1
Z||000019||SLYGUY||10-19-2001||12:36 AM||||I'm glad that my local Pontiac dealer is a pro. I ordered my OEM cluch master cylinder at 12:30pm Wednesday and it was here at 8:30am Thursday. To say that I was surprised was an understatement. I honestly didn't expect it till Friday or Monday. <BR>Not all dealers are so shady.<P>Sly||||reg||1
Z||000020||rogergarrison||10-19-2001||08:51 AM||||I work daily with dealerships, expecially the parts depts, with my business. I find theres no middle ground, either they totally suck or there absolutly awesome. Caddy seems the best (Columbus Cadillac) and local Pontiac dealer is the worst (Dave Gill Pontiac).||||reg||1
Z||000021||Old Lar||10-19-2001||10:19 AM||||I have had good luck with my Pontiac dealer. (Ralph Pontiac-Honda) I bought my 87 GT there in 1987. The owner has a loaded 88 GT for his Florida car with 11K miles on it. The parts department has been great. The service department has been great. But at $64/hr labor rather pricey.  They painted my 88 GT and have done several paint touch ups on my 87.  But its the people that work there that make the dealership work.||||reg||1
